# Postgres container configuration — NOT API configuration.
#
# The shape of $APP_DIR/db/.env on the server, beside docker-compose.prod.yml. Docker
# Compose reads a file called `.env` from the compose file's own directory
# automatically, which is why `docker compose up -d` needs no inline vars
# and — more importantly — why a later `docker compose up -d` or a host reboot
# cannot come back up with different credentials than the volume was created with.
#
# You do not write it: the first API deploy does (ensure_database in deploy.yml),
# deriving all three values from DATABASE_URL — so the two sides start in sync — and
# never rewrites an existing one. The API reaches Postgres through DATABASE_URL alone.
# Each value below appears in exactly one place in the URL:
#
#   DATABASE_URL="postgresql://cv-builder:<password>@127.0.0.1:5009/cv-builder?schema=public"
#                              ^^^^^^^^^^ ^^^^^^^^^^                ^^^^^^^^^^
#                              POSTGRES_USER  │                     POSTGRES_DB
#                                      POSTGRES_PASSWORD
#   (goes in server/.env.production.local)
#
# `cv-builder` twice is correct — the role and the database share a name, as in dev.
# In SQL the hyphen means both names are written in double quotes: "cv-builder".
# `?schema=public` is a different thing again: the schema INSIDE that database.
# Prisma defaults to `public`; leave it alone.
#
# 127.0.0.1, never `localhost` — it can resolve to ::1, where nothing listens.
# 5009 is the HOST port this container publishes; inside it Postgres is still on 5432.
#
# Dev publishes 5009 too, so a dev and a production DATABASE_URL differ only by
# host and password. `127.0.0.1` vs `localhost` is the tell.
#
# ⚠ POSTGRES_PASSWORD is only read on FIRST start, when the data directory is
# initialised. Changing it later does nothing — the volume keeps the original.
# To rotate, change it inside Postgres instead:
#   docker exec -it cv-builder-postgres-prod psql -U cv-builder -d cv-builder
#   ALTER USER "cv-builder" WITH PASSWORD 'new';
# then update DATABASE_URL and redeploy.

POSTGRES_USER=cv-builder
# Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
# Hex on purpose — no characters that need percent-encoding inside DATABASE_URL.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
POSTGRES_DB=cv-builder
